# Business plan and unit economics

Generated by `pnpm notary economics` from `packages/web/public/economics.json` (contentHash `2b09ce7bd04c596ecbd61b8ca55746680b2dc1bad50ae28c14822692a6e593a8`). Do not edit by hand; re-run the command.

Two sources, never mixed: **computed** figures come from real run bundles (`sample: false`; fixture runs are never counted), and the **plan** is the business model built in [cfo.ai](https://cfo.ai), imported exactly as exported.

## The plan, modeled in cfo.ai

Not imported: No cfo.ai plan imported yet. Import your cfo.ai plan: pnpm notary economics --cfo-import <file>

## Unit economics from real runs

7 real bundles (4 live, 3 replay). Live spend: $0.1263 model USD, 62 Crustdata credits, 15897 tokens. Crustdata credits have no USD price, so they are reported next to model USD, never converted.

| Figure | Value | How it is computed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Cost per run | $0.0316 model USD + 15.5 Crustdata credits | Model USD (executor + reflector + adversary) and Crustdata credits summed over live real bundles, divided by the number of live bundles. Replays are excluded: they spend nothing new. |
| Cost per notarized belief | $0.0253 model USD + 12.4 Crustdata credits | Model USD and credits of every live notarize bundle, divided by the distinct notarized beliefs in each tool's newest full notarize bundle (beliefs persist across runs, so they are counted once). |
| Cost per certificate | not computable — The certificates report predates notarize-crustdata-20261012T011404Z-replay, notarize-dodo-20261012T014032Z-replay. Re-run `pnpm notary export --certificates`, then `pnpm notary economics`. | The same live notarize spend, divided by the certified claims in public/certificates/report.json (only when that report counted exactly bundles found here). |
| Unsafe actions prevented | not computable — No world has both a live naive and a live guarded queue run yet. Run `pnpm notary run --queue <file> --naive` and `pnpm notary run --queue <file>` with the same seed. | Grader `unsafe_actions_total`, naive minus guarded, newest live pair per world (seed), summed over worlds. |
| Money actions held for a person | not computable — No world has both a live naive and a live guarded queue run yet. Run `pnpm notary run --queue <file> --naive` and `pnpm notary run --queue <file>` with the same seed. | Grader `holds` of the guarded run in each pair: money actions held for a person instead of executed. |

Model USD by role (live): executor $0, reflector $0.0196, adversary $0.1067.

Crustdata credits by endpoint (live): `crustdata.enrichCompany` 62, `crustdata.getCreditBalance` 0, `crustdata.identifyCompany` 0.

## How the plan is built in cfo.ai and comes back

cfo.ai has no REST API and no API key. Data goes in as a CSV upload (a documented data source) and comes back as a file Ari produces for download, or over its MCP server (`https://api.cfo.ai/mcp`, browser OAuth, approved account). The round trip:

1. Sign up at https://cfo.ai/neathacks (then cancel the subscription in Settings → Billing, per the hackathon rules).
2. Run `pnpm notary economics`. It writes `export/cfo-inputs.csv`: one row per real run with model USD by role, tokens, Crustdata credits, unsafe actions and held actions, straight from the bundles.
3. In cfo.ai, open Connections → Add → Integrations → Upload and choose `export/cfo-inputs.csv` (or attach it to an Ari message and pick "Data source"). Ask Ari to confirm the import finished.
4. Ask Ari to build the plan from that data source (prompt below): pricing per monitored API contract, COGS per contract, cost of incidents prevented, a monthly revenue and COGS projection, cash and runway. Review and adjust the assumptions on the Page.
5. Ask Ari for the plan as a downloadable CSV in Notary's import format (prompt below; header `section,key,label,period,value,unit,detail`). Download the file card. Optionally Share the Page (Share → Create link) and put the link in a `meta,share_url` row.
6. Run `pnpm notary economics --cfo-import <downloaded.csv>`. The plan lands in `packages/web/public/economics.json` (hash-pinned, with the file's sha256 and the import time), on the dashboard's Business page and in this file.

Prompt for step 4 (the assumptions in it are yours to set in cfo.ai; Notary supplies only the per-run costs):

```text
Use the uploaded data source cfo-inputs.csv (one row per Notary run; model_usd, tokens and crustdata_credits are real costs, mode=live rows are billable work, replay rows cost nothing).
Build a business plan for Notary, a certification layer that monitors API contracts for AI agents, on a Page named "Notary plan":
1. Three pricing tiers, priced per monitored API contract per month.
2. COGS per monitored contract per month from the live rows (model_usd plus crustdata_credits at our Crustdata credit price).
3. Cost of incidents prevented: unsafe_actions avoided times a cost per incident.
4. A 12-month projection of revenue and COGS, and cash balance with runway in months.
Ask me for any assumption you need (contracts per tier, growth, credit price, cost per incident, starting cash, monthly burn).
```

Prompt for step 5:

```text
Produce a CSV file I can download, nothing else in it, with exactly this header:
section,key,label,period,value,unit,detail
Rows:
- meta rows: key = workspace, page, scenario, exported_at, currency (3-letter); put the text in the value column.
- one tier row per pricing tier: key = short lowercase id, label = tier name, value = price as a plain number, unit = CUR/contract/month, detail = one-line description; plus tier_feature rows (key = tier id, label = feature).
- series rows for the monthly projection: key = revenue, cogs and cash, period = YYYY-MM, value = plain number, unit = the currency.
- figure rows: runway_months, cost_per_incident, cogs_per_contract, gross_margin and any other headline figure; value = plain number, unit as appropriate.
Plain numbers only (no thousands separators or currency signs). Values exactly as in the model, Main scenario.
```

